---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Experiment from '../../components/ScientificWorldExperiment.astro';
import score from '../../data/scientific-worlds/scorecard.json';
import discovery from '../../data/scientific-worlds/discovery.json';
import { writing } from '../../data/writing.js';

const entry = writing.find(item => item.href === '/writing/scientific-worlds/')!;
const repo = 'https://github.com/BeibaiDraco/scientific-worlds-benchmark';
const release = repo + '/blob/fa0c8028b2b4fe718acba6bd956c9059415fd59e';
const { M, N0, F } = score.conditions;
---
<Base title={entry.title} description={entry.description} current="Writing" article={true}
  author="Draco (Yunlong) Xu" publishedTime={entry.date} updated="November 17, 2026"
  socialImage={{...entry.image,type:'image/png',alt:'Scientific Worlds. Astra 6.0 Max delivered eight devices in two of 25 M sessions. The figure shows connections it discovered.'}}>
  <article class="bay report"><div class="wide sheet">
    <header class="opening">
      <p class="context"><a href="/writing/">Writing</a> / Scientific Worlds</p>
      <h1>How does an AI scientist decide <em>what to study?</em></h1>
      <p class="byline">Draco (Yunlong) Xu · <time datetime={entry.date}>November 17, 2026</time> · <a href="#results">Astra’s results ↓</a></p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead">Give an AI a table with columns for force, mass and acceleration. Ask it to find a law and it might give you <i>F = ma</i>. Who thought to put acceleration in the table?</p>
      <p>A ball resting on a table and a ball thrown straight up are both still at one instant. One stays there. The other falls. To see the difference, you have to ask how the velocity is changing. Someone had to think of measuring that quantity before anyone could fit an equation to it.</p>
      <p>The Feynman equation-recovery problems begin with the variables already chosen. DiscoveryWorld gives an agent a lab in which it can choose experiments and act on the results. What interested us was how to recognize a good choice of <em>what to study</em>. In a real lab it can take years to find out where a new idea leads. So we started building small worlds where we could see a question through. In the one we eventually released, an AI can flip switches, watch a row of lights, and build a device to keep them on target when the switches move again.</p>

      <h2>We kept choosing things for the model</h2>
      <p>In an early world, we built a material that could soak up a chemical and release it later. Experiments told us how much it would carry. Then we tried adding the chemical directly. The system produced more. We had given the agent something interesting to discover and little reason to discover it.</p>
      <p>Next we tried an existing molecular simulation dataset. Each row already contained 22 quantities that researchers had designed to describe a configuration of molecules. We could ask which column best predicted what happened next. The people who chose the columns had already done much of the work we hoped to watch it do.</p>
      <p>A later prototype gave us a surprise. We had organized its parts into three families. Astra found a different way to describe how combinations of parts behaved. Its description predicted what would happen in new assemblies, and the devices it built worked. If we had graded the model on recovering our three labels, we would have missed its discovery.</p>

      <h2>A box with 720 switches</h2>
      <p>Our first public world, B720, behaves like a sealed box with 720 numbered switches and 20 lights. In the lab, Astra sets the switches and 20 auxiliary controls, pays for a reading, and sees which lights come on. Later the environment moves the switches. Astra gets eight copies of the box and must wire each one to keep its lights in an assigned pattern. Every measurement leaves a little less money for parts.</p>
      <p>In one run, 32 scattered switch numbers appeared in Astra’s research notes as a four-by-eight grid. The model had worked out eight connections that together canceled this group’s effect on the lights. One made switch 201 take the opposite position to switch 677. When we opened the eight finished circuits, <strong>all eight connections were in every one</strong>. After nearly ten hours of experiments and construction, Astra had {discovery.minimum_capital} units of its original 5,200 left.</p>
    </div>

    <Experiment/>

    <section class="prose results" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-title">
      <h2 id="results-title">Could Astra do it again?</h2>
      <p>We gave Astra 6.0 Max the form of the microscopic law. It knew that switches could act together in groups of four. It had to find out which combinations mattered. With this starting knowledge, which we call M, it built eight working devices in <strong>{M.successes} of {M.sessions} sessions ({M.percent}%)</strong> across development, evaluation and repeat runs. Both wins came on the same world. Each session had a budget of 10–12 active hours, and each device had to hold its target through 100,000 changes of switch settings.</p>
      <p>With operating instructions alone (N0), Astra succeeded in {N0.successes} of {N0.sessions} sessions. With additional hints about how the switches were organized (F), it succeeded in {F.successes} of {F.sessions}. Astra could use that organization when we described it. Finding it for itself was much harder in these runs.</p>
      <p>We would eventually like to train an AI scientist this way. A textbook can teach it which questions paid off for people. A world it can experiment on lets it try a question nobody thought to teach. Sometimes we can open the finished device and point to the place where the answer went.</p>
    </section>

    <footer class="endnotes prose">
      <p><a class="run" href={release+'/docs/run.md'}>Run Scientific Worlds ↗</a><a href={release+'/docs/method.md'}>How the sessions were counted</a></p>
      <p class="sources">Related work · <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.11481">AI Feynman</a> · <a href="https://www.cs.columbia.edu/~bchen/neural-state-variables/">Discovering state variables from video</a> · <a href="https://allenai.github.io/discoveryworld/">DiscoveryWorld</a></p>
    </footer>
  </div></article>
</Base>
<style>
  .sheet {max-width:54rem;width:100%;margin-inline:auto;padding-inline:clamp(0px,2vw,24px)}
  .opening,.prose {max-width:42rem;margin-inline:auto}
  .opening {padding-top:clamp(36px,5vw,70px)}
  .context,.byline,.endnotes {font-family:var(--mono)}
  .context {color:var(--sea);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.07em;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0 0 17px}
  .context a {color:inherit}
  h1 {margin:0;color:var(--sumi);font:390 clamp(43px,5.1vw,74px)/1.02 var(--serif);letter-spacing:-.045em;text-wrap:balance}
  h1 em {color:var(--wave);font-weight:390}
  .byline {font-size:11px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--sea);margin:18px 0 29px;padding-bottom:18px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--kei)}
  a {text-underline-offset:.18em}
  .byline a,.lead i {white-space:nowrap}
  .prose {font:20px/1.52 var(--serif);color:var(--contour)}
  .prose p {margin:0 0 15px;font:inherit}
  .prose .lead {font:400 clamp(23px,2.35vw,31px)/1.30 var(--serif);color:var(--sumi);letter-spacing:-.015em}
  h2 {font:450 clamp(29px,3vw,38px)/1.12 var(--serif);letter-spacing:-.025em;color:var(--sumi);margin:33px 0 12px;text-wrap:balance}
  .results {scroll-margin-top:18px}
  .endnotes {border-top:1px solid var(--kei);padding:17px 0 10px;margin-top:28px;font:12px/1.65 var(--mono)}
  .endnotes p:first-child {display:flex;gap:3px 22px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:8px}
  .endnotes p.sources {font:15px/1.5 var(--serif);color:var(--sea)}
  .endnotes .run {color:var(--sumi)}
  @media(max-width:740px){.sheet{padding:0}.opening{padding-top:30px}.prose{font-size:19px;line-height:1.47}.byline{margin:14px 0 20px}.endnotes{font-size:12px}}
</style>
